{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 26,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.065,
            0.378,
            0.082
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.121,
            0.884,
            0.163
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Brahmagupta’s Rules for Subtraction (Brāhma-sphuṭa-siddhānta 18.31-18.32)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.167,
            0.884,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": "1. If a smaller positive is subtracted from a larger positive, the result is positive (e.g., 3 – 2 = 1)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.213,
            0.885,
            0.256
          ],
          "content": "2. If a larger positive is subtracted from a smaller positive, the result is negative (e.g., 2– 3 = – 1)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.259,
            0.884,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": "3. Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding the corresponding positive number (e.g., 2 – (– 3) = 2 + 3)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.304,
            0.884,
            0.346
          ],
          "content": "4. Subtracting a number from itself gives zero (e.g., 2 – 2 = 0 and – 2 – (– 2) = 0)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.349,
            0.885,
            0.416
          ],
          "content": "5. Subtracting zero from a number gives the same number (e.g., – 2 – 0 = – 2 and 0 – 0 = 0). Subtracting a number from zero gives the number’s inverse (e.g., 0 – (– 2) = 2)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.429,
            0.885,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": "Once you understand Brahmagupta’s rules, you can do addition and subtraction with any numbers whatsoever — positive, negative, and zero!"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.515,
            0.353,
            0.539
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.546,
            0.884,
            0.589
          ],
          "content": "1. Can you explain each of Brahmagupta’s rules in terms of Bela’s Building of Fun, or in terms of a number line?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.209,
            0.591,
            0.617,
            0.612
          ],
          "content": "2. Give your own examples of each rule."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.627,
            0.886,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": "Brahmagupta was the first to describe zero as a number on an equal footing with positive numbers as well as with negative numbers, and the first to give explicit rules for performing arithmetic operations on all such numbers, positive, negative, and zero—forming what is now called a ring. It would change the way the world does mathematics."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.763,
            0.887,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "However, it took many centuries for the rest of the world to adopt zero and negative numbers as numbers. These numbers were transmitted to, accepted by, and further studied by the Arab world by the 9th century, before making their way to Europe by the 13th century."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.083,
            0.942,
            0.116,
            0.959
          ],
          "content": ""
        },
        {
